(function () {
  window.AIPO = window.AIPO || {};
  window.AIPO.lessons = window.AIPO.lessons || [];
  window.AIPO.scenarios = window.AIPO.scenarios || {};
  window.AIPO.why = window.AIPO.why || {};
  window.AIPO.questions = window.AIPO.questions || [];
  window.AIPO.cards = window.AIPO.cards || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }

  window.AIPO.lessons.push(
    {"id":"risk-1","n":1,"domain":"risk","title":"Seven questions every AI product should answer","body":"NIST AI RMF 1.0 describes seven characteristics of trustworthy AI: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair, with harmful bias managed. A PO can read them as seven plain questions. Does it work under real conditions? Can it endanger people? Can it withstand attack? Can we see who decided what? Can people make sense of outputs? Does it protect personal data? Does it treat groups fairly?\n\nThe characteristics influence each other, so the work is balancing trade-offs, not maximising each one. NIST gives examples: a highly secure but unfair system is undesirable, and so is an accurate system that nobody can interpret. Privacy techniques can sometimes cost accuracy. A transparent system is not automatically accurate, secure or fair, although an opaque one is hard to judge on any of them. A system in which harmful bias has been reduced is not necessarily fair, for instance if it stays inaccessible to some people.\n\nThe MEASURE function asks that risks to safety, security and resilience, transparency and accountability, explainability, privacy and fairness, as identified in the MAP function, are evaluated and documented. NIST also says the decision to commission or deploy an AI system should rest on a contextual assessment of these characteristics and the relative risks, impacts, costs and benefits, informed by a broad set of interested parties. The PO brings product context to that assessment and makes sure the trade-offs chosen are written down and agreed with the people accountable for the risk.","points":["Name all seven characteristics and give one product example for each.","Valid and reliable means it works as intended under realistic conditions, not only in a demo.","Safe includes the ability to shut down, modify or put a human into a system that deviates.","Transparency says what happened, explainability says how, interpretability says why and what it means.","The characteristics trade off against each other, so record the balance you chose and who agreed."],"traps":["Optimising one characteristic, such as accuracy, and ignoring the rest.","Assuming a transparent system must also be fair or secure.","Declaring a system fair because harmful bias was reduced in one metric.","Leaving trade-off decisions to engineers without the people accountable for the risk."],"sources":["NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), section 3 (3.1 to 3.7)","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MEASURE 2.6 to 2.11"],"sourceVersion":"AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023","reviewed":false},
    {"id":"risk-2","n":2,"domain":"risk","title":"The twelve generative AI risks in plain language","body":"NIST AI 600-1, the Generative AI Profile, defines twelve risks that are unique to or made worse by generative AI. Knowing the names lets a PO spot them in a product review. Confabulation is confidently stated but false output. Information integrity covers content that blurs fact and fiction or helps large-scale misinformation. Information security covers lowered barriers for attackers and attacks on the system itself. Data privacy covers leakage or unauthorised use of personal or sensitive data. Intellectual property covers easier copying of protected content and exposure of trade secrets.\n\nHarmful bias and homogenization covers amplified historical biases, performance gaps between groups or languages, and outputs that all look alike. Human-AI configuration covers the way people and systems interact, including automation bias, over-reliance, anthropomorphising and emotional entanglement. Value chain and component integration covers untraceable or poorly vetted third-party components such as datasets and pre-trained models. Environmental impacts cover the energy and resource cost of training and running models.\n\nThree risks are handled here at name-and-define level only. Dangerous, violent or hateful content is easier production of violent, inciting or hateful material. CBRN information or capabilities is easier access to dangerous information about chemical, biological, radiological or nuclear weapons. Obscene, degrading and abusive content is easier production of abusive imagery. If any of these could apply, involve your security, safety and legal specialists early. Which risks matter depends on the model, system and use, so check each against your actual product.","points":["Learn the twelve names so you can run a quick check of any generative product against them.","Confabulation, information integrity and human-AI configuration often combine: wrong output, trusted too much, spread widely.","Value chain risk is about knowing what third-party data and models sit inside your product.","Data privacy, intellectual property and information security are the risks most likely to need specialist teams.","For CBRN, abusive and violent content risks, name them, assess whether they could apply, and escalate."],"traps":["Assuming an internal-only tool is outside the list.","Treating confabulation as a rare bug rather than a known risk to design for.","Ignoring risks that sit in components you did not build.","Discussing sensitive content risks in operational detail instead of escalating to specialists."],"sources":["NIST AI 600-1 (Jul 2024), section 2 (the twelve risks)","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), section 3.3 and 3.6","General practice, not from a source: use the list as a review prompt"],"sourceVersion":"AI 600-1, Jul 2024","reviewed":false},
    {"id":"risk-3","n":3,"domain":"risk","title":"Size the harm, choose a response, record what remains","body":"Risk work starts by asking what could go wrong and for whom. NIST MAP 5.1 asks that the likelihood and magnitude of each identified impact, both beneficial and harmful, are identified and documented. The evidence can include expected use, past uses of similar systems, public incident reports and feedback from people outside the team. Harms can fall on individuals, groups, communities, organisations and society, so a business-only view is incomplete.\n\nNot every risk can be treated at once. MANAGE 1.2 asks that treatment is prioritised by impact, likelihood and the resources or methods available. A low-likelihood risk with severe impact still deserves attention. For risks judged high priority, MANAGE 1.3 asks that responses are developed, planned and documented. The options it lists include mitigating, transferring, avoiding or accepting; NIST gives no definitions, so the examples below are general practice: mitigating reduces the likelihood or impact, for instance through human review; transferring shifts some cost or responsibility, for instance by contract, but harm to users may remain; avoiding means not doing the risky thing; accepting is a conscious choice to live with the risk.\n\nWhatever you choose, something remains. MANAGE 1.4 asks that negative residual risks, defined as the sum of all unmitigated risks, are documented for downstream acquirers and end users. For safety risks, MEASURE 2.6 adds that the system to be deployed should be demonstrated safe and its residual negative risk should not exceed the risk tolerance. As practical judgement, acceptance is a decision for the people accountable for the risk, which the next lesson covers.","points":["Estimate both likelihood and magnitude, for benefits and harms, and write them down.","Use several evidence sources, including people outside the team and past incidents.","Prioritise by impact, likelihood and available resources, not by what is cheapest or loudest.","The response options include mitigate, transfer, avoid or accept, each documented.","Residual risk is what remains after treatment and is documented for end users and downstream acquirers."],"traps":["Listing benefits in the business case and omitting harms.","Treating a contract clause as if it removes harm to affected people.","Accepting a risk informally with no record.","Calling a risk mitigated while never stating what is left."],"sources":["NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 5.1","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MANAGE 1.2, 1.3 and 1.4","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MEASURE 2.6","General practice, not from a source: the plain-language examples of each response"],"sourceVersion":"AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023","reviewed":false},
    {"id":"risk-4","n":4,"domain":"risk","title":"Who decides how much risk is acceptable","body":"Risk tolerance is the amount of risk an organisation is willing to take on. NIST MAP 1.5 asks that organisational risk tolerances are determined and documented, and GOVERN 1.3 asks for processes that set the needed level of risk management activity based on that tolerance. GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for decisions about risks associated with AI development and deployment. In practice, a Product Owner stays accountable for the product decision under the Scrum Guide, but does not set risk tolerance alone. The PO brings evidence, options and the product context, and the people accountable for the risk decide with them.\n\nGOVERN 1.1 asks that legal and regulatory requirements involving AI are understood, managed and documented. Laws and regulations may apply to your product, and what they require depends on place, sector and use. A PO is not the right person to give legal conclusions. As practical judgement, when you are unsure whether a use of data or a feature is permitted, pause that part and bring in your legal, compliance or privacy team, with a clear description of the data, the purpose and the users.\n\nGOVERN 1.2 asks that the characteristics of trustworthy AI are built into policies, processes and practices, and GOVERN 1.4 asks for transparent policies and controls based on risk priorities. Check what your organisation already has before inventing your own process.","points":["Risk tolerance is determined and documented, not left implicit.","NIST ties the needed level of risk management activity to the stated risk tolerance; as practical judgement, higher stakes call for more.","Executive leadership takes responsibility for risk decisions, with the PO supplying evidence and options.","When unsure about legal or regulated data, pause that use and involve legal, compliance or privacy.","Use the organisation's existing policies and controls first."],"traps":["Setting the tolerance yourself to unblock the team.","Treating a vendor's compliance statement as the answer to a legal question.","Proceeding with a pilot first and asking the privacy team afterwards.","Giving stakeholders a legal opinion instead of routing the question."],"sources":["NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.5","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), GOVERN 1.1 to 1.4","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), GOVERN 2.3","Scrum Guide (Nov 2020), Product Owner accountability","General practice, not from a source: pause and involve specialists when unsure"],"sourceVersion":"AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023","reviewed":false},
    {"id":"risk-5","n":5,"domain":"risk","title":"Misuse, security and a route for people to push back","body":"Security and misuse are product risks, not only engineering ones. NIST describes common security concerns for AI as adversarial examples (inputs crafted to fool a model), data poisoning (tampering with training data) and exfiltration of models, training data or other intellectual property through system endpoints. It also notes that resilience reaches to unexpected or adversarial use, abuse or misuse of the model or data. MEASURE 2.7 asks that security and resilience, as identified in MAP, are evaluated and documented. As practical judgement, a PO should ask security specialists to test for misuse before wide release, and should plan monitoring and a way to limit or switch off the feature if abuse appears.\n\nPeople affected by a system need a way to be heard. MEASURE 3.3 asks for feedback processes for end users and impacted communities to report problems and appeal system outcomes, integrated into the evaluation metrics. GOVERN 5.1 asks for policies to collect, consider, prioritise and integrate feedback from people outside the team about individual and societal impacts. A route that nobody owns, or whose findings never reach the team, does not meet the intent.\n\nCulture matters too. GOVERN 4.1 asks for a critical thinking and safety-first mindset, and GOVERN 4.2 asks teams to document risks and impacts and communicate them more broadly. GOVERN 6.1 and 6.2 add policies for third-party risks and contingency plans for failures in high-risk third-party data or AI systems.","points":["Name adversarial examples, data poisoning and exfiltration as common AI security concerns.","Test for misuse before wide release and keep a way to limit or switch off the feature.","Give affected people a route to report problems and appeal outcomes.","Assign an owner to feedback and track appeal outcomes as an evaluation measure.","Plan for failures in third-party data or models you depend on."],"traps":["Relying on terms of use to stop misuse.","Setting up an appeal route that nobody reviews.","Counting only upheld appeals as a signal.","Waiting for real losses before testing the system for abuse."],"sources":["NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), section 3.3","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MEASURE 2.7 and 3.3","NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), GOVERN 4.1 and 4.2, 5.1, 6.1 and 6.2","General practice, not from a source: test for misuse, monitor, and keep a way to limit or switch off the feature"],"sourceVersion":"AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023","reviewed":false}
  );

  window.AIPO.scenarios.scRK1 = {"title":"A clinic network's message assistant","text":"A regional clinic network plans an assistant that drafts replies to patient portal messages and summarises message threads for staff. The assistant would see message history that includes health details, and it runs on a hosted model supplied by a third party. Staff are meant to review each draft before it is sent. Leadership wants to launch this quarter. The team's risk list so far contains one item, accuracy, and there is no route for patients to report problems or appeal."};
  window.AIPO.scenarios.scRK2 = {"title":"A retailer's refund scoring model","text":"A retailer's loyalty team builds a model that scores customers on return behaviour and automatically refuses refunds above a set limit. It is trained on past returns and enriched with data from an outside provider. Customers who are refused receive no explanation. A public chat assistant that answers refund questions has been shown to be easy to manipulate. Leadership would like to automate all refusals to cut cost."};

  window.AIPO.questions.push(
    S("rk001", "risk", "A PO reviews a slide that lists the seven trustworthiness characteristics from NIST AI RMF 1.0. Which list is correct?", ["Valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; fair with harmful bias managed","Accurate and fast; safe; scalable and cheap; secure; transparent; privacy-enhanced; fair and unbiased in all cases for all users","Valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; compliant and certified; profitable and scalable; fair with bias managed","Valid and reliable; efficient; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; easy to use; fair and unbiased"], 0, "The seven are valid and reliable, safe, secure and resilient, accountable and transparent, explainable and interpretable, privacy-enhanced, and fair with harmful bias managed."),
    S("rk002", "risk", "A retailer's assistant answers well in testing but starts failing when real customers phrase requests in unfamiliar ways. Which characteristic is most directly in question?", ["Privacy-enhanced","Explainable and interpretable","Valid and reliable","Accountable and transparent"], 2, "NIST ties validity to meeting requirements for the intended use and warns about poor generalisation beyond training conditions."),
    S("rk003", "risk", "A PO adds a stop control and a path for a human to step in when an assistant behaves outside expected limits. Which characteristic does this mainly support?", ["Fair with harmful bias managed","Safe","Privacy-enhanced","Explainable and interpretable"], 1, "Safety practices include real-time monitoring and the ability to shut down, modify or intervene."),
    S("rk004", "risk", "An engineer proposes a privacy technique that slightly lowers accuracy in a setting where accuracy matters. What is the best PO response?", ["Choose accuracy and drop the privacy technique, since accuracy is the main characteristic in this case","Adopt the strongest privacy technique in every case and accept whatever accuracy loss follows","Treat it as a trade-off: assess it in context with accountable people, then decide and document","Leave the choice to the engineers because it is a technical matter for them to settle"], 2, "A comprehensive approach balances trade-offs among the trustworthiness characteristics."),
    M("rk005", "risk", 2, "Which TWO statements about the seven characteristics are consistent with NIST AI RMF 1.0?", ["They influence each other, so the trade-offs need balancing","Once harmful bias has been reduced, the system is by definition fair","Meeting one characteristic fully removes the need to assess the others","A transparent system is not automatically accurate, secure or fair","Only the engineering team needs to judge them"], [0,3], "The characteristics are interdependent and each needs attention."),
    S("rk006", "risk", "A tool's predictions are balanced across demographic groups, but its interface cannot be used with assistive technology. What does this show?", ["The system is fair, because predictions balanced across demographic groups are the definition of fairness","Accessibility is a usability matter for the design team and sits outside fairness concerns","Fairness depends only on the training data and not at all on how it is used","Less bias does not make it fair: access and wider disparities still matter"], 3, "Fairness is broader than demographic balance."),
    S("rk007", "risk", "A drafting assistant states an invented policy detail with complete confidence. Which of the 12 generative AI risks is this?", ["Confabulation","Information integrity","Intellectual property","Value chain and component integration"], 0, "AI 600-1 defines confabulation as confident but false content that may mislead users."),
    S("rk008", "risk", "Staff start accepting every suggestion without checking, and some users treat the assistant like a friend. Which risk is this?", ["Human-AI configuration","Harmful bias and homogenization","Information security","Environmental impacts"], 0, "Human-AI configuration is about the human side of the interaction."),
    S("rk009", "risk", "A team uses a third-party model and a purchased dataset without knowing where either came from or how they were vetted. Which risk is most directly raised?", ["Data privacy","Confabulation","Dangerous, violent or hateful content","Value chain and component integration"], 3, "The value chain risk concerns procured datasets, pre-trained models and similar components."),
    M("rk010", "risk", 2, "Which TWO of the following are among the 12 risks named in NIST AI 600-1?", ["Slow response time under heavy load","Licence cost overruns across several vendors","Information integrity","Low user adoption after the first month","Harmful bias and homogenization"], [2,4], "The twelve are specific, named risks, not general product concerns."),
    S("rk011", "risk", "Which description best matches the CBRN information or capabilities risk in AI 600-1?", ["Generative AI easing access to information on chemical, biological, radiological or nuclear weapons","Leakage or unauthorised use of sensitive personal or health data through the system","Confidently stated false claims about scientific topics in everyday answers","Unclear provenance of third-party components used inside the product"], 0, "CBRN is handled at name-and-define level only in this course."),
    S("rk012", "risk", "A team says its internal help assistant is out of scope for the 12 risks because no customers will use it. What is the best response?", ["Agree, since the twelve risks only apply to products that customers and outsiders use directly","Check all 12 against the real use, since internal tools can still raise risks such as privacy and over-reliance","Agree, because internal users can be trained","Check only the risks the model vendor lists as relevant to its model"], 1, "Which risks matter depends on the specific model, system and use case."),
    S("rk013", "risk", "What does NIST MAP 5.1 ask to be identified and documented for each impact?", ["Only the cost to the project of harmful impacts","Likelihood and magnitude, for benefits and harms","Only the single most severe impact, with likelihood omitted","The vendor's liability for each impact"], 1, "Both beneficial and harmful impacts are sized."),
    S("rk014", "risk", "A feature has a low likelihood of harm, but the harm to an individual would be severe. How should the PO treat it?", ["Weigh impact and likelihood together, not likelihood alone","Ignore it, since likelihood alone decides priority in risk work","Defer it until the first incident shows that it is real","Treat it the same as every other low-likelihood risk on the list"], 0, "Priority reflects impact, likelihood and available resources or methods."),
    S("rk015", "risk", "The team can treat only some of its documented risks this quarter. Which basis for prioritising matches MANAGE 1.2?", ["Whichever is cheapest and quickest to fix first","Whichever was found first in the earliest review","Impact, likelihood and the resources or methods available","Whichever the loudest stakeholder raised in the last meeting"], 2, "Prioritise treatment by impact, likelihood and available resources or methods."),
    M("rk016", "risk", 2, "Which TWO are listed in NIST MAP 5.1 as bases for estimating likelihood and magnitude of impacts?", ["The trend in the team's sprint velocity across recent releases","Past uses of AI systems in similar contexts","Feedback from people outside the team that built or deployed it","Competitor feature comparisons gathered at a planning workshop","Developer preference for one design over another in a review"], [1,2], "Use varied evidence, including outside views and past incidents."),
    S("rk017", "risk", "Which set names the risk response options in NIST MANAGE 1.3?", ["Mitigating, transferring, avoiding or accepting","Ignoring, delaying, delegating or documenting","Detecting, reporting, fixing or retiring","Mitigating, monitoring, escalating or archiving"], 0, "Responses to high-priority risks are developed, planned and documented."),
    S("rk018", "risk", "A PO decides to drop an automated-decision feature because the harms to individuals outweigh the benefits. Which response is this?", ["Mitigating the risk","Transferring the risk","Accepting the risk","Avoiding the risk"], 3, "Avoidance means not doing the risky thing. This is a general practice definition; MANAGE 1.3 lists avoiding among the response options."),
    S("rk019", "risk", "A team adds human review and a stricter threshold to reduce a risk, though some risk remains. What is the right description and next step?", ["Avoidance, since some of the risk was removed","Transfer, since the reviewers now share the work","Acceptance, so nothing more needs recording","Mitigation, with the residual risk documented"], 3, "Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact (general practice definition). MANAGE 1.4 asks that negative residual risks, the sum of all unmitigated risks, are documented for downstream acquirers and end users."),
    S("rk020", "risk", "How does NIST MANAGE 1.4 describe negative residual risk, and for whom is it documented?", ["Risk that a vendor absorbed under contract, documented for the finance team","Risk found after release, documented for the support and operations teams","The sum of all unmitigated risks, documented for acquirers and end users","Risk ranked low, documented only when someone formally accepts it"], 2, "Residual risk stays visible to the people who rely on or are affected by the system."),
    M("rk021", "risk", 2, "Which TWO belong in the documented response to a high-priority risk?", ["The negative residual risk that remains for end users and downstream acquirers","A promise that the risk cannot occur","Only the technical fix, without the reasoning","The chosen option among mitigating, transferring, avoiding or accepting","The names of developers who will be held to blame"], [0,3], "A good record names the response and what remains."),
    S("rk022", "risk", "A PO is unsure whether a planned use of customer data is permitted. What is the best action?", ["Proceed with a small pilot and then ask for sign-off afterwards","Ask the model vendor to confirm it is fine","Pause that use and ask legal, compliance or privacy","Rely on a colleague's memory of how a similar project went"], 2, "As practical judgement, pause and escalate when unsure."),
    S("rk023", "risk", "What does GOVERN 1.1 say should be true of legal and regulatory requirements involving AI?", ["They are met automatically by buying a vendor product","They are understood, managed and documented","They are handled after launch by support teams","They are left to individual developers to interpret"], 1, "Laws and regulations may apply, so route questions to the right experts."),
    S("rk024", "risk", "A team wants to add patients' health details to prompts for personalisation. What should the PO do first?", ["Treat it as possibly regulated data and involve privacy and compliance first","Remove the names, assume the data is then anonymous, and proceed with the personalisation work","Test it on a few live users to see if problems arise","Ask only the security team, since privacy is a security topic"], 0, "Health details are sensitive and may be regulated, so involve your privacy, compliance and legal teams before any use of them."),
    M("rk025", "risk", 2, "Which TWO are sound PO actions when a feature may touch regulated data?", ["Decide using summaries read online and record your own conclusion","Describe the data, purpose and users clearly for the privacy and legal teams","Release to a small group first so issues show up","Hold release of that capability until they have reviewed it","Leave the data fields out of the documentation to simplify review"], [1,3], "Specialists decide the legal view; the PO supplies facts and holds the line."),
    S("rk026", "risk", "Users of an eligibility tool say they were wrongly refused and have no way to contest it. Which NIST measure expectation is missing?", ["A higher accuracy target for the next model release and a longer test period","A regular refresh schedule for the training data used by the model","A communications plan that explains the product to the people affected","Ways for affected people to report problems and appeal outcomes"], 3, "Affected people need a way to report problems and appeal outcomes."),
    M("rk027", "risk", 2, "Which TWO are common security concerns that NIST names for AI systems?", ["Backlog items growing too large to refine well","Low user engagement in the first weeks","Adversarial examples","Exfiltration of models or training data through system endpoints","Slow stakeholder approvals at each release"], [2,3], "NIST names adversarial examples, data poisoning and exfiltration."),
    S("rk028", "risk", "A PO notices that users can feed hidden instructions to an assistant so it ignores its rules. What is the best action?", ["Update the user guide to ask users politely not to do this in future","Wait to see whether anyone abuses it once it is live in production","Treat it as a security risk: involve security specialists and add tested controls","Accept it because only a few users would ever try it, and move on"], 2, "Plan testing, monitoring and a way to limit or switch off the feature."),
    S("rk029", "risk", "Whose feedback does GOVERN 5.1 ask the organisation to collect, consider, prioritise and integrate?", ["Only the team that built or deployed it and its business sponsors","People outside the team, on individual and societal impacts","Only the executive sponsors who approved the budget for it","Only the regulators who supervise the sector it serves"], 1, "Outside feedback widens the view of impacts."),
    S("rk030", "risk", "An appeal route exists, but nobody reviews appeals and findings never reach the team. What is the best fix?", ["Assign an owner, track appeal outcomes and feed the results into risk review","Nothing, because having an appeal route is enough protection","Let the model answer appeals itself to save staff effort","Count only the appeals that are upheld and ignore the rest as noise"], 0, "Feedback must reach people who can act."),
    S("rk031", "risk", "Who should set the risk tolerance for an AI product?", ["The PO alone, because the PO is accountable for the value of the product","People accountable for the risk, such as executives, with the PO bringing evidence","The development team, since it works closest to the system day to day","The model vendor, since it knows the model better than anyone else"], 1, "Risk tolerance is set with the right people, not by one role."),
    S("rk032", "risk", "What does NIST MAP 1.5 say about organisational risk tolerances?", ["They stay informal to preserve flexibility","They are determined and documented","They are set after release from incident counts","They are copied from another team without review"], 1, "Documented tolerance lets teams judge residual risk against it."),
    S("rk033", "risk", "One product is a low-stakes internal summariser and another advises on patient scheduling. What follows from risk tolerance?", ["The same full process for both products, whatever their very different stakes and users","No risk work at all for the internal summariser","Each needs effort matched to its stakes, likely more for the scheduling advisor","Each team picks its own level of effort without any common process"], 2, "Scale effort to stakes through a defined process."),
    S("rk034", "risk", "Executives ask the PO whether a high residual risk is acceptable before launch. What is the best PO contribution?", ["Decide quietly so the launch is not delayed by debate","Present evidence so accountable leaders can decide, and record it","Say it is a technical matter for the engineering team to settle","Recommend acceptance without showing the evidence behind it"], 1, "Accountable leaders decide risk; the PO informs and documents."),
    S("rk035", "risk", "Patient health details would be sent to a hosted third-party model. Which pair of the 12 risks is most directly raised?", ["Environmental impacts, and intellectual property","Harmful bias and homogenization, and information integrity","Data privacy, and value chain and component integration","Information security, and environmental impacts"], 2, "Check the data flow and the components together.", "scRK1"),
    S("rk036", "risk", "Which approach gives the strongest view of the harm from wrong or misleading drafts?", ["Count the minutes saved per message across the staff team","Use the vendor's published accuracy statement for the hosted model as the main evidence","Assess the harm only after pilot users start to complain about drafts","Size likelihood and magnitude using similar past uses and outside input"], 3, "Use varied evidence including outside views.", "scRK1"),
    S("rk037", "risk", "The team wants to store message threads to improve the assistant later. What should the PO do?", ["Store them for now and decide later how the data will be used","Store only the threads that staff rate as useful","Pause the plan and ask privacy, legal and compliance first","Ask the vendor if storing is allowed"], 2, "Pause and involve specialists when data use is uncertain.", "scRK1"),
    S("rk038", "risk", "Staff review drafts before sending. What additional route should be in place?", ["A note in the portal terms and conditions saying drafts may sometimes contain errors","A route for patients and staff to report problems and appeal, tracked as a measure","A yearly satisfaction survey sent to all patients","A suggestion box that the team may read when time allows"], 1, "Feedback must be easy, owned and tracked.", "scRK1"),
    S("rk039", "risk", "Which option is a genuine mitigation of the risk that confident wrong drafts reach patients?", ["Require trained staff to review each draft, with a flag for uncertain ones","Add a clause to the vendor contract that places the blame for any errors on the vendor","Record the risk in the meeting minutes and then move on","Remove the review step to speed up replies"], 0, "Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact.", "scRK1"),
    S("rk040", "risk", "Executives want to launch this quarter but the risk list is incomplete. What is the best PO move?", ["Launch as asked, since leadership has set the date","Flag the gaps and ask accountable leaders to decide timing","Quietly drop the unfinished items from the risk list","Delay the launch on the PO's own authority and tell no one"], 1, "Escalate with facts and let the accountable people decide.", "scRK1"),
    S("rk041", "risk", "Refused customers cannot get any explanation of why a refund was declined. Which characteristic is most directly lacking?", ["Safe","Secure and resilient","Explainable and interpretable","Valid and reliable"], 2, "Explainability and interpretability help people make sense of outputs.", "scRK2"),
    S("rk042", "risk", "Testing shows refusal rates are much higher in some postal areas. What is the best next step?", ["Accept it, since overall accuracy is high and the average customer is treated well","Remove the postal field and assume the bias is gone with it","Ask the data provider to certify its data","Examine it as possible harmful bias and document the fairness results"], 3, "Evaluate fairness and bias and document results.", "scRK2"),
    M("rk043", "risk", 2, "The public chat can be manipulated to approve refunds. Which TWO actions fit best?", ["Have security specialists test the chat for manipulation","Rely on terms of use that forbid any manipulation of the chat by customers","Wait for refund losses to show whether the problem matters at all","Ask the vendor to confirm in writing that the chat is safe","Add monitoring and a way to limit or switch off the chat"], [0,4], "Test for misuse and keep a way to stop.", "scRK2"),
    S("rk044", "risk", "Leadership wants every refusal fully automated. Which response best reduces harm while keeping value?", ["Mitigate: flag cases for staff review and add a customer appeal route","Accept: automate all refusals and handle any complaints as they arrive","Transfer: have the data provider bear the cost of customer complaints","Avoid: cancel the whole project without assessing any smaller options first"], 0, "Choose a response that fits impact and benefit.", "scRK2"),
    S("rk045", "risk", "The outside data provider's enrichment includes personal details of customers. What should the PO do?", ["Keep using it, because the provider supplied it","Use only the fields the team itself judges non-sensitive","Ask the developers to document the data source later","Pause use and raise it with privacy, legal and compliance"], 3, "Pause and involve specialists when data use is uncertain.", "scRK2"),
    S("rk046", "risk", "After mitigation, a small risk of wrongful refusals remains. What must happen with it?", ["Nothing further, since small risks do not need a record","Place it in a technical appendix where few people will read it","Announce to customers that the remaining risk has been fully eliminated","Document it and have accountable leaders confirm it is within tolerance"], 3, "Residual risk is documented for end users and acquirers.", "scRK2")
  );

  Object.assign(window.AIPO.why, {
    "rk001": ["It matches the seven characteristics NIST describes in section 3.","It swaps in engineering and commercial qualities such as speed and cost, which are not among the seven.","It replaces explainability and interpretability with compliance and profit, which are not characteristics in the framework.","Efficiency and usability are not characteristics in the framework, and the safe and privacy-enhanced entries are missing or altered."],
    "rk002": ["Privacy is about protecting personal information, which is not the issue described.","Explainability helps people understand outputs, but here the outputs are failing, not hard to understand.","Validity and reliability concern working as intended under real conditions of use, including settings beyond the original tests.","Transparency is about information being available, not about whether the system performs under varied input."],
    "rk003": ["Fairness concerns equitable treatment and bias, not stop controls.","NIST lists the ability to shut down, modify or put a human into a deviating system among practical approaches to safety.","Privacy is about protecting personal information.","Explainability is about making sense of outputs, not stopping the system."],
    "rk004": ["No characteristic is automatically the main one, and dropping privacy for accuracy ignores the trade-off.","Maximising one characteristic without looking at context is the mistake NIST warns about.","NIST says the characteristics trade off and that privacy techniques can sometimes cost accuracy, so the balance is a documented decision with the right people.","The trade-off affects users and risk, so the people accountable for the risk should be involved, not only engineers."],
    "rk005": ["NIST states that trade-offs among the characteristics call for balancing.","NIST notes that systems with harmful bias mitigated are not necessarily fair.","The characteristics interact, so each still needs assessment.","NIST notes that transparency does not guarantee the other qualities.","NIST says decisions should be informed by a broad set of interested parties."],
    "rk006": ["NIST says a system with harmful biases mitigated is not necessarily fair.","NIST cites inaccessibility to people with disabilities as a fairness concern.","Fairness also depends on use, access and wider systemic factors, not only training data.","NIST gives this kind of example: balanced predictions can still leave a system inaccessible to some people or worsen disparities."],
    "rk007": ["Confabulation is confidently stated but erroneous or false output.","Information integrity is about content that blurs fact and fiction at scale or supports misinformation campaigns, a related but wider risk.","Intellectual property concerns copying of protected content.","Value chain risk concerns untraceable or poorly vetted third-party components."],
    "rk008": ["This risk covers how people and systems interact, including automation bias, over-reliance, anthropomorphising and emotional entanglement.","Harmful bias concerns amplified biases and group performance gaps.","Information security concerns attacks and lowered barriers for attackers.","Environmental impacts concern energy and resource use."],
    "rk009": ["Data privacy may also be affected, but the described issue is unknown provenance of components.","Confabulation is about false output, not component sourcing.","Harmful content is a different risk about what the system produces.","Untraceable or poorly vetted third-party components reduce transparency and accountability for downstream users."],
    "rk010": ["Latency matters to users but is not one of the twelve.","Cost control matters but is not one of the twelve.","It is one of the twelve and covers content that blurs fact and fiction or enables misinformation.","Adoption is a product outcome, not one of the twelve risks.","It is one of the twelve and includes outputs that become undesirably uniform."],
    "rk011": ["This is the name-level definition of the risk. If a product could touch this area, escalate to security and safety specialists.","That describes the data privacy risk.","That describes confabulation.","That describes value chain and component integration."],
    "rk012": ["AI 600-1 does not limit the risks to customer-facing products.","The risks depend on the model, system and use, so each should be checked against the actual product, internal or not.","Training helps but does not remove risks such as privacy leakage or over-reliance.","The vendor's view is not a substitute for checking your own use and data."],
    "rk013": ["Cost to the project alone misses harms to people and omits likelihood.","MAP 5.1 covers likelihood and magnitude of each identified impact, beneficial and harmful.","Omitting likelihood and other impacts leaves the picture incomplete.","Liability is not what MAP 5.1 asks for."],
    "rk014": ["MANAGE 1.2 prioritises by impact and likelihood together, with available resources.","Ignoring impact is the mistake; both factors count.","Waiting for an incident puts the harm after the fact.","Different impacts call for different priority."],
    "rk015": ["Cost alone ignores how serious the risk is.","Order of discovery says nothing about priority.","MANAGE 1.2 names impact, likelihood and available resources or methods.","Volume of complaint is not a risk measure."],
    "rk016": ["Velocity measures delivery pace, not harm.","MAP 5.1 names past uses in similar contexts.","MAP 5.1 names feedback from those external to the team.","Competitor features do not indicate likelihood or size of harm.","Preference is not evidence of impact."],
    "rk017": ["These are the four response options named.","Ignoring and delaying are not valid responses.","These are lifecycle activities, not response options.","Monitoring, escalating and archiving are not among the named options."],
    "rk018": ["In general practice, mitigation keeps the activity and reduces its likelihood or impact.","In general practice, transfer shifts some cost or responsibility to another party.","In general practice, acceptance means proceeding and living with the risk.","Not doing the risky activity is avoidance."],
    "rk019": ["Avoidance means not doing the risky activity at all.","Sharing work with reviewers is not transferring the risk.","Even accepted residual risk should be recorded.","Mitigation reduces risk, and MANAGE 1.4 asks that negative residual risks are documented."],
    "rk020": ["Contract transfer is a different idea, and finance is not the named audience.","Timing of discovery does not define residual risk.","That is the definition given, with those two groups named.","Residual risk is not limited to low-ranked items."],
    "rk021": ["MANAGE 1.4 asks that residual risk is documented for those groups.","Risks are reduced, not guaranteed away.","Reasoning and decision context are part of a useful record.","MANAGE 1.3 asks that responses are developed, planned and documented using these options.","Assigning blame is not a documentation requirement and discourages reporting."],
    "rk022": ["A pilot still uses the data and puts the harm before the check.","A vendor sales view is not a substitute for your own legal and privacy review.","When unsure about legal or regulated data use, pause and involve the right specialists, with the data and purpose described.","Memory of another project is not a reliable basis for a legal question."],
    "rk023": ["Buying a product does not make your use compliant.","GOVERN 1.1 asks that these requirements are understood, managed and documented.","Waiting until after launch puts compliance after the harm.","Interpretation belongs with those who have the expertise and accountability."],
    "rk024": ["Health data may be regulated, so the privacy and compliance teams should review the use before it starts.","Removing names may not make data anonymous, and the team should not decide alone.","Live testing exposes real people before the review.","Security is involved, but privacy and compliance bring different expertise."],
    "rk025": ["The PO should not replace specialist advice with own reading.","Clear facts let specialists give a sound view.","A limited release still exposes real people.","Pausing the affected capability avoids harm before the check.","Hiding information defeats the review."],
    "rk026": ["Accuracy alone does not give people a route to contest a decision.","Refreshing data does not create an appeal route.","Communication is useful but is not a route to report and appeal.","MEASURE 3.3 asks for feedback and appeal processes integrated into the evaluation metrics."],
    "rk027": ["That is a delivery concern, not an AI security concern.","Engagement is a product measure.","NIST lists adversarial examples among the concerns.","NIST lists this exfiltration among the concerns.","Approval pace is a process concern."],
    "rk028": ["A request in a guide does not stop determined misuse.","Waiting puts the harm before the response.","Misuse is a security risk that needs specialist testing, controls and a switch-off option.","Rarity of attempts does not justify acceptance without the accountable people."],
    "rk029": ["The build team already knows its own view.","GOVERN 5.1 is about feedback from those external to the team regarding individual and societal impacts.","Sponsors are a narrow group.","Regulators are not the only external voice."],
    "rk030": ["MEASURE 3.3 asks that feedback and appeals are integrated into evaluation metrics, which needs ownership and follow-through.","A route nobody reads gives no protection.","Removing human review of appeals defeats the purpose.","Counting only upheld appeals hides valid signals."],
    "rk031": ["The PO stays accountable for the product decision but does not decide risk tolerance alone.","GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for decisions about AI risks, and the PO supplies evidence and context.","Developers inform the decision but do not own the risk.","A vendor does not carry the organisation's risk."],
    "rk032": ["Informal tolerances cannot be checked or applied consistently.","MAP 1.5 asks that tolerances are determined and documented.","Setting them after release puts the target after the result.","Copying without review ignores context."],
    "rk033": ["Identical effort ignores differing stakes.","Low stakes may reduce the effort but do not remove it.","GOVERN 1.3 links the needed level of risk management activity to the organisation's risk tolerance.","Without a common process, tolerance is not applied consistently."],
    "rk034": ["Quiet decisions skip the accountable people.","The PO supplies evidence and options and the accountable people decide, with a record kept.","Residual risk is a business decision, not only technical.","A recommendation without evidence cannot be challenged."],
    "rk035": ["Neither named risk is the main issue here.","Those risks may matter elsewhere but not for this data flow.","Health details raise data privacy, and relying on a third-party model raises value chain concerns.","Information security may apply, but environmental impacts do not fit the question."],
    "rk036": ["Time saved shows benefit, not harm.","A vendor statement is not evidence from your context.","Waiting for complaints puts the harm first.","MAP 5.1 asks for likelihood and magnitude based on such evidence."],
    "rk037": ["Collecting first and deciding later puts the harm before the check.","Selecting by usefulness does not address permission or privacy.","Health information is sensitive, so the specialists should review before storing begins.","The vendor cannot decide the organisation's obligations."],
    "rk038": ["A note does not give people a way to report or appeal.","MEASURE 3.3 asks for feedback and appeal processes integrated into evaluation.","A survey is too slow and broad to catch problems.","An unowned box lacks follow-through."],
    "rk039": ["Human review before the harm reduces the likelihood of wrong drafts reaching patients.","A contract line may shift cost but does not reduce harm to patients.","Recording alone does not reduce the risk.","Removing review raises the risk."],
    "rk040": ["Following a request without sharing the gaps hides the risk.","The accountable people can only decide well if they see the gaps.","Dropping risks hides them from decision makers.","The PO does not decide risk tolerance alone."],
    "rk041": ["Safety concerns endangerment, not explanations.","Security concerns attack and misuse.","Explainability helps people understand how and why a decision was made.","Validity concerns working as intended, not explanation."],
    "rk042": ["An average can hide harm to groups.","Bias can persist through other features and processes.","A certification does not replace your own evaluation.","Overall accuracy can hide group differences, and bias has several sources."],
    "rk043": ["Specialist testing finds misuse paths early.","Terms do not stop abuse.","Waiting puts losses first.","A vendor assurance is not testing of your system.","Monitoring and a switch-off limit the damage."],
    "rk044": ["Human review and an appeal route reduce harm while keeping benefits.","Complaints after the fact leave harm uncontrolled.","A provider cannot take on harm to customers.","Avoidance is an option but cancelling without assessing alternatives is too blunt."],
    "rk045": ["Supply by a provider does not settle permission.","The team should not decide sensitivity alone.","Documentation after use comes too late.","Third-party data may carry privacy and rights issues, so specialists should review before use."],
    "rk046": ["Small residual risk still affects people.","Hiding it removes transparency.","The risk remains, so claiming otherwise is false.","Residual risk is documented and judged against tolerance by the accountable people."]
  });

  window.AIPO.cards.push(
    {"id":"rkc01","domain":"risk","front":"What are the seven NIST trustworthiness characteristics?","back":"Valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; fair with harmful bias managed.\nTrap: forgetting that they trade off, so maximising one can weaken another."},
    {"id":"rkc02","domain":"risk","front":"Give a product example of valid and reliable.","back":"An assistant tested on realistic, varied customer requests, with results documented and monitored after launch.\nTrap: calling a system reliable because it did well in a demo."},
    {"id":"rkc03","domain":"risk","front":"What does safe mean for an AI product, and what is one practical safety measure?","back":"It should not endanger human life, health, property or the environment. One practical measure is the ability to shut down, modify or put a human into a system that deviates.\nTrap: treating safety as a final test instead of designing it in early."},
    {"id":"rkc04","domain":"risk","front":"How do security and resilience differ?","back":"Resilience is the ability to withstand or recover from unexpected adverse events. Security includes resilience and also covers avoiding, protecting against, responding to and recovering from attacks.\nTrap: treating them as the same thing."},
    {"id":"rkc05","domain":"risk","front":"How do transparency, explainability and interpretability differ?","back":"Transparency answers what happened, explainability answers how a decision was made, interpretability answers why and what it means to the user.\nTrap: assuming a transparent system is therefore explainable or fair."},
    {"id":"rkc06","domain":"risk","front":"What does privacy-enhanced mean and what can it cost?","back":"Design choices that protect autonomy, identity and dignity, such as data minimising and de-identification. Some techniques can reduce accuracy.\nTrap: treating privacy as a legal checkbox rather than a design trade-off."},
    {"id":"rkc07","domain":"risk","front":"Is a system with harmful bias reduced automatically fair?","back":"No. Fairness is broader, including access, disparities and context.\nTrap: declaring victory after one balanced metric."},
    {"id":"rkc08","domain":"risk","front":"Name the 12 generative AI risks in AI 600-1.","back":"CBRN information or capabilities; confabulation; dangerous, violent or hateful content; data privacy; environmental impacts; harmful bias and homogenization; human-AI configuration; information integrity; information security; intellectual property; obscene, degrading and abusive content; value chain and component integration.\nTrap: assuming a risk does not apply because the tool is internal."},
    {"id":"rkc09","domain":"risk","front":"What is confabulation?","back":"Confidently stated but erroneous or false output that can mislead users.\nTrap: treating it as a rare bug rather than a known design risk."},
    {"id":"rkc10","domain":"risk","front":"What is human-AI configuration risk?","back":"Risks from how people and the system interact: automation bias, over-reliance, anthropomorphising and emotional entanglement.\nTrap: assuming a human in the loop removes the risk."},
    {"id":"rkc11","domain":"risk","front":"What is value chain and component integration risk?","back":"Untraceable or poorly vetted third-party components, such as datasets and pre-trained models, that reduce transparency and accountability.\nTrap: assuming the vendor has vetted everything."},
    {"id":"rkc12","domain":"risk","front":"How do information integrity and information security differ?","back":"Information integrity is about content that blurs fact and fiction or supports misinformation. Information security is about lowered barriers for attackers and attacks on the system.\nTrap: treating false content and cyber attack as one risk."},
    {"id":"rkc13","domain":"risk","front":"What is the data privacy risk for generative AI?","back":"Leakage or unauthorised use, disclosure or de-anonymisation of personal or sensitive data.\nTrap: sending sensitive data to a tool before the privacy team has reviewed it."},
    {"id":"rkc14","domain":"risk","front":"What is harmful bias and homogenization?","back":"Amplified historical or societal biases, performance gaps between groups or languages, and uniform outputs that skew decisions.\nTrap: checking only the average result."},
    {"id":"rkc15","domain":"risk","front":"How should a PO treat the CBRN and abusive content risks?","back":"Know their names and definitions, assess whether the product could touch them, and escalate to security, safety and legal specialists. Do not explore them in operational detail.\nTrap: trying to test or discuss them without specialists."},
    {"id":"rkc16","domain":"risk","front":"What are the intellectual property and environmental impact risks?","back":"Intellectual property: easier copying of protected content and exposure of trade secrets. Environmental: the energy and resource cost of training and running models.\nTrap: ignoring both because they feel remote from user harm."},
    {"id":"rkc17","domain":"risk","front":"What does MAP 5.1 ask the team to size for each impact?","back":"The likelihood and magnitude, for beneficial and harmful impacts, using evidence such as past similar uses and outside feedback.\nTrap: sizing benefits only."},
    {"id":"rkc18","domain":"risk","front":"What are the four risk response options?","back":"Mitigate, transfer, avoid or accept, with the chosen response documented for high-priority risks.\nTrap: treating a contract clause as removing harm to people."},
    {"id":"rkc19","domain":"risk","front":"What is residual risk and who is it documented for?","back":"The sum of all unmitigated risks, documented for downstream acquirers and end users.\nTrap: stopping the record once a mitigation is added."},
    {"id":"rkc20","domain":"risk","front":"Who decides risk tolerance for an AI product?","back":"The people accountable for the risk, such as executive leadership, with the PO supplying evidence and options.\nTrap: the PO setting a number alone to unblock the team."},
    {"id":"rkc21","domain":"risk","front":"When should a PO involve legal, compliance or privacy teams?","back":"When a feature, data use or market may be subject to laws or regulations, or when unsure. Describe the data, purpose and users, and pause the affected use until reviewed.\nTrap: asking after a pilot has started."},
    {"id":"rkc22","domain":"risk","front":"What does MEASURE 3.3 ask for?","back":"Feedback processes for end users and impacted communities to report problems and appeal outcomes, built into evaluation metrics.\nTrap: an appeal route nobody owns or reads."}
  );
})();
